diff --git a/fuzz/README.md b/fuzz/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..453a420
--- /dev/null
+++ b/fuzz/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# Parser fuzzing (patch 0070)
+
+`victory-fuzz` is a backend workspace member. Its four `&[u8]` consumers in
+`src/lib.rs` are called unchanged by both libFuzzer and ordinary Rust tests.
+No input is executed, no target opens title files, and no panic is caught by
+our consumers. Parser errors remain typed and include byte offsets.
+
+## Stable CI, without cargo-fuzz
+
+From the reconstructed backend workspace:
+
+```powershell
+cargo test --locked -p victory-fuzz --lib -- --nocapture
+cargo test --locked -p victory-formats -p victory-pe
+cargo test --locked -p victory-shader dxbc::tests::
+```
+
+Plain `cargo test` also includes `victory-fuzz` as a workspace member. The
+consumer byte-compares every disk seed against its source generator, checks
+its expected acceptance/rejection, tries 200 deterministic mutations per seed,
+and tries every shorter prefix of valid seeds. Mutation choices include
+truncation, bit flips, zero/FF fields, insertion, and deletion. Any panic fails
+the test; errors must carry offsets no greater than the input length.
+
+From the SCARLETT overlay, `python scripts/test_parsers.py` reconstructs the
+ordered patches and runs that consumer; the overlay CI invokes it directly.
+To prepare a persistent workspace first:
+
+```powershell
+python scripts/test_parsers.py --prepare-only --checkout target/native/fuzz-source
+cd target/native/fuzz-source
+```
+
+## Coverage-guided runs
+
+Install a nightly toolchain and cargo-fuzz (do not change your default toolchain):
+
+```powershell
+rustup toolchain install nightly --profile minimal
+cargo install cargo-fuzz --locked
+cargo +nightly fuzz run --features fuzzing rpf9 fuzz/corpus/rpf9 fuzz/seeds/rpf9 -- -max_total_time=60 -max_len=4096
+cargo +nightly fuzz run --features fuzzing bcpack fuzz/corpus/bcpack fuzz/seeds/bcpack -- -max_total_time=60 -max_len=4096
+cargo +nightly fuzz run --features fuzzing shader_header fuzz/corpus/shader_header fuzz/seeds/shader_header -- -max_total_time=60 -max_len=4096
+cargo +nightly fuzz run --features fuzzing pe fuzz/corpus/pe fuzz/seeds/pe -- -max_total_time=60 -max_len=4096
+```
+
+Create the four `fuzz/corpus/<target>` directories before running. libFuzzer
+writes new discoveries to the first corpus directory; keep committed seeds
+unchanged. `--features fuzzing` enables optional `libfuzzer-sys`; stable CI has
+no C++/nightly dependency. Use `-runs=20000 -seed=70` for bounded repeatable
+smoke runs. A 4096-byte smoke limit is a campaign choice, not a parser bound;
+raise it for long-list/large-output campaigns. Failures go under ignored
+`fuzz/artifacts/`; minimize with `cargo +nightly fuzz tmin --features fuzzing
+<target> <artifact>` and promote only regenerated synthetic reproductions.
+
+AddressSanitizer is the cargo-fuzz default. See the official
+[Rust Fuzz Book setup](https://rust-fuzz.github.io/book/cargo-fuzz/setup.html)
+and [Windows setup](https://rust-fuzz.github.io/book/cargo-fuzz/windows/setup.html).
+Windows additionally needs MSVC C++ tools and C++ AddressSanitizer, with its
+runtime DLL directory on PATH. Do not count an unsanitized run as ASan evidence.
+
+## Synthetic corpus and bounds
+
+Regenerate with `cargo run --locked -p victory-fuzz --example write_seeds`.
+`src/seeds.rs` writes all bytes from small source structures and synthetic
+entropy streams produced by the BCPack unit-test encoder. Nothing is copied
+from a title. The asset policy exempts only these explicitly named fixtures;
+adding a seed requires its source generator, expected result, and policy entry.
+
+| Target | Seeds cover | Production bound |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `rpf9` | empty/split prefix, short header, reversed extents, huge full length | borrowed table envelope, 16 MiB maximum prefix, zero allocations |
+| `bcpack` | valid BC1/3/4/5/6/7, truncation, unsupported format, output-count limit | input/output/record buffer each <=64 MiB, checked multiplication |
+| `shader_header` | empty/one-part DXBC, short table, header overlap, huge offset/size/count | <=256 borrowed parts; checked offset arithmetic |
+| `pe` | named and ordinal imports, delay imports, export aliases, forwarders, invalid strings/counts/sections/directory extents, missing TLS terminator | <=1024 sections, <=4096 modules per import table, <=65536 imports total, <=65536 export addresses/names, <=4096 bytes/string, <=1024 callbacks, 16 MiB cumulative logical output budget |
+
+BCPack has no self-describing size: its **fuzz framing only** is `format:u8`,
+`output_blocks:u16le`, then packed bytes. The consumer rejects counts outside
+1..=256 before allocating (<=4096 output bytes); the production decoder's
+separate 64 MiB bounds apply to callers too. BCPack offsets refer to the next
+packed byte consumed when a stream/block error was detected (plus three in
+the consumer), not an invented uncompressed offset. PE errors point to the
+file field/read; dependency header errors identify the start of the attempted
+header. Logical PE output charges are conservative for strings, but allocator
+capacity can exceed the charge by a bounded constant factor.
+
+## Known-unsound / unproven scope
+
+No new unsafe code is used in these parsers or consumers. Finite fuzz runs
+are evidence, not a proof for every input or allocator/OS failure.
+
+* There was **no native RPF9 table reader** in the baseline. The reference
+  probe establishes only `9FPR`, full/partial prefix lengths at offsets 4/8,
+  and `16 <= partial <= full <= file length`. The new reader validates that
+  envelope and returns opaque table regions. Entry decoding, encryption,
+  checksums and the fourth header word remain unknown. Full RPF9 archive
+  parsing is **not covered or claimed safe**.
+* The shader target parses the DXBC header/part table, not DXIL payloads,
+  decompression, ISA decoding, translation or driver validation. It does not
+  authenticate digests or reject overlapping bodies; borrowed slices remain
+  bounded even when two parts share bytes.
+* BCPack uses immutable built-in tables. Arbitrary replacement table files,
+  semantic agreement with hardware, BC2, and huge-output campaigns are outside
+  these targets. Its fixed table reader asserts the established exact schema
+  after validating file length; that is a real format invariant.
+* PE targets analyze PE32+ AMD64 file bytes, including both alias modes. They
+  do not map or execute code, apply relocations, or parse COFF import libraries.
+  Directory/string/count ceilings are deliberate policy limits and may reject
+  unusual valid images. Import directory sizes must include the terminating
+  descriptor; permissive Windows readers may accept underspecified sizes.
+* Other existing unsafe loader/GPU/diagnostic paths are outside this work.
+  A successful parser result does not establish those paths are safe.
